  <h1>Types of Nursing Home Neglect</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Aspen Lockwood (AL), Editor-in-Chief. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>Pressure sores (decubitus ulcers)</h2><p>Caused by prolonged pressure on bony prominences when residents aren&rsquo;t repositioned. Stage 3/4 pressure sores in nursing home residents are presumptively caused by neglect. One of the most commonly litigated nursing home claims.</p>
  <h2>Falls and fall-related injuries</h2><p>Failure to assess fall risk, implement a fall prevention plan, provide adequate supervision, or maintain a safe environment. Falls causing hip fractures in the elderly are frequently fatal or permanently disabling.</p>
  <h2>Malnutrition and dehydration</h2><p>Failure to provide adequate nutrition and fluids, monitor weight loss, or respond to declining intake. Residents with dementia or swallowing disorders are particularly vulnerable. Malnutrition requiring hospitalization is a strong indicator of systemic neglect.</p>
  <h2>Medication errors</h2><p>Wrong medication, wrong dose, wrong patient, or failure to monitor for dangerous interactions. Medication errors are among the leading causes of preventable harm in long-term care facilities.</p>
  <h2>Physical and emotional abuse</h2><p>Hitting, restraining, belittling, threatening, or sexually abusing residents. Often unreported because residents fear retaliation or lack capacity to report. Strongest punitive damage cases in nursing home litigation.</p>
